1
00:00:06,860 --> 00:00:13,260
Welcome to the laboratory. This video demonstrates two key techniques in microbiology, aseptic

2
00:00:13,260 --> 00:00:18,380
technique and the streak plate technique. Aseptic technique basically means to create

3
00:00:18,380 --> 00:00:23,740
and maintain a clean sterile environment for you to work in. This is extremely important

4
00:00:23,740 --> 00:00:28,600
when working with microorganisms because you want to be sure that the organism you're

5
00:00:28,600 --> 00:00:35,340
transferring to the plate or a broth is your organism of interest without any contaminants.

6
00:00:35,340 --> 00:00:40,040
So to create a sterile environment, we firstly need to wipe down the surface that we're working

7
00:00:40,040 --> 00:00:46,580
on. So you should have some bench cleaner. So spray the bench with the bench cleaner.

8
00:00:46,580 --> 00:00:51,820
Bench cleaner is basically disinfectant which will remove any dust or unwanted organisms

9
00:00:51,820 --> 00:00:57,420
that may be on the bench. So wipe down the disinfectant with a tissue or some paper

10
00:00:57,420 --> 00:01:04,300
towel provided and place that in the bin. A key piece of equipment that you need to

11
00:01:04,340 --> 00:01:09,500
use to create a sterile environment is the Bunsen burner. When the Bunsen is lit, the flame

12
00:01:09,500 --> 00:01:14,540
creates an area of sterility in the air and also keeps the bench nice and clean as well.

13
00:01:14,540 --> 00:01:20,340
So you want to light that soon after you've wiped your bench down. So grab your matches,

14
00:01:20,340 --> 00:01:26,940
strike your match and then ensure that the hole of the Bunsen is closed and then you

15
00:01:26,940 --> 00:01:31,580
can turn on your tap. You should hear the gas coming out and then you can light your flame

16
00:01:31,980 --> 00:01:39,410
and your match can be disposed of in the pipette bucket. So when you're not directly using your

17
00:01:39,410 --> 00:01:44,370
flame, you should have it on the orange flame just so that you're aware that it's there and

18
00:01:44,370 --> 00:01:51,150
that you don't accidentally wave your hand or arm through it. So the technique that I'm going

19
00:01:51,150 --> 00:01:59,070
to demonstrate is the street plate technique. So this involves transferring an organism whether it be

20
00:01:59,070 --> 00:02:08,270
from a solid medium or from a broth culture onto another solid medium. So in this example,

21
00:02:08,270 --> 00:02:16,350
I'm going to use nutrient agar and I'm transferring the organism E. coli. One of the first things you

22
00:02:16,350 --> 00:02:22,110
need to do when performing the street plate technique is to actually label your plate.

23
00:02:22,750 --> 00:02:28,030
So you should have a permanent marker on your bench and you need to label your plate with some

24
00:02:28,030 --> 00:02:34,670
key information. Because this is a nutrient agar plate, I'm going to label the plate with NA,

25
00:02:34,670 --> 00:02:40,430
the abbreviation for nutrient agar and you should also include your initials from your first name,

26
00:02:40,430 --> 00:02:46,590
your middle name and your last name. And in this example, I'm transferring E. coli. So you

27
00:02:46,590 --> 00:02:54,940
can write the organism name or you could write the exercise number that this plate refers to.

28
00:02:54,940 --> 00:03:07,090
You must also include the date in your labeling. So you should always label your plate on the bottom

29
00:03:07,090 --> 00:03:13,970
of the plate just in case the lid is lost in transit or is removed from the plate. So you

30
00:03:13,970 --> 00:03:18,930
would always label on the bottom and you also need to label it on the edge or the circumference

31
00:03:18,930 --> 00:03:27,070
of the plate so it doesn't obstruct the view of what is growing on your plate. The street plate

32
00:03:27,070 --> 00:03:34,990
technique is actually shown in a diagram in your manual. So this is figure one. To perform the

33
00:03:34,990 --> 00:03:41,870
street plate technique, it requires you to use a sterile loop and to transfer some bacteria or

34
00:03:41,870 --> 00:03:49,870
yeast onto a plate and then to continue streaking it. So basically the purpose of the street plate

35
00:03:49,870 --> 00:03:55,550
technique is to dilute the number of cells so that when you get to your quaternary lines,

36
00:03:55,550 --> 00:04:02,270
you should have individual colonies. So one viable cell from a bacteria or yeast usually

37
00:04:02,270 --> 00:04:09,390
gives rise to one colony. So in this diagram, you can see in the quaternary steps that there

38
00:04:09,390 --> 00:04:14,830
are streak lines which don't intersect with the tertiary lines. That is one way of performing the

39
00:04:14,830 --> 00:04:20,990
street plate technique. With your quaternary lines, you could also perform a vertical line

40
00:04:20,990 --> 00:04:24,990
and a squiggle into the middle of the plate. There are a number of ways that you can do

41
00:04:25,070 --> 00:04:36,050
this and both ways are acceptable. So to transfer your organism, you want to make sure that your

42
00:04:36,930 --> 00:04:42,290
Bunsen flame is turned to the blue flame so that you can see the apex of the flame.

43
00:04:43,970 --> 00:04:50,210
You will need your loop and you're going to sterilize it in the flame. So basically the

44
00:04:50,210 --> 00:04:55,890
loop is a piece of wire with a loop on the end and you want to make sure that the entire

45
00:04:56,770 --> 00:05:02,530
surface of the loop, including the stem, is sterile. So you basically hold it in the blue

46
00:05:02,530 --> 00:05:09,170
flame and you can see it turns orange when it gets hot and you want to make sure the stem

47
00:05:09,170 --> 00:05:15,410
and the loop is sterile. Once that's sterilized, you need to let it cool for a few seconds. So

48
00:05:15,410 --> 00:05:22,530
just hold it near your Bunsen flame. A way of determining whether the loop is cool enough

49
00:05:22,530 --> 00:05:28,450
is to actually touch it to the agar surface of the plate. So the loop needs to be cool because

50
00:05:28,450 --> 00:05:33,330
if it's very hot, it can actually kill the bacteria or yeast that you put onto your

51
00:05:33,330 --> 00:05:40,370
loop and you don't want them to be killed. So in this example, I have some E.coli on a

52
00:05:40,370 --> 00:05:45,730
solid medium. So now my loop is sterile and cool enough, I'll just check by touching it

53
00:05:45,730 --> 00:05:52,930
to the surface of the plate. It didn't sizzle so it's ready to be used. So you want to collect

54
00:05:54,530 --> 00:06:03,250
a single colony from this plate and transfer it onto your new sterile agar plate. So remember,

55
00:06:03,250 --> 00:06:07,890
when you labeled your plate, it was upside down so you need to turn it now the right way up.

56
00:06:09,570 --> 00:06:14,610
If you feel uncomfortable when streaking, you can actually draw the streak lines as per your

57
00:06:14,610 --> 00:06:19,970
diagram onto the bottom of the plate. For this demonstration, I'm not going to do that because it

58
00:06:19,970 --> 00:06:27,330
will obstruct the view. So now I have my colony on my loop. I'm going to perform the primary streaks.

59
00:06:27,890 --> 00:06:34,050
So basically you move your loop with the bacteria on it back and forth onto the surface of the agar

60
00:06:34,050 --> 00:06:39,810
and that has to be done very gently so that you don't tear the agar. The agar is hard,

61
00:06:39,810 --> 00:06:44,930
but it is like a jelly substance and it can easily tear. So once you've transferred it,

62
00:06:44,930 --> 00:06:52,050
you need to flame your loop again to ensure that any excess bacteria is removed. Remember,

63
00:06:52,050 --> 00:06:57,330
the purpose of the streak plate technique is to dilute the cells. So we're removing any of

64
00:06:57,330 --> 00:07:03,650
the cells that might have been on our loop. So you need to make sure that you turn the plate

65
00:07:03,650 --> 00:07:08,690
so that you can perform your secondary set of streak lines. So they must intersect with

66
00:07:08,690 --> 00:07:13,890
the primary ones. So by turning the plate, that helps you to remember that you need to

67
00:07:13,890 --> 00:07:21,170
intersect them. So you need to drag the loop across the surface of the agar, this time lifting it off

68
00:07:21,170 --> 00:07:28,480
at each of the strokes and then replace the lid. And then again, you need to flame your loop.

69
00:07:29,280 --> 00:07:32,160
So it's really important when you're streaking as well that you

70
00:07:33,200 --> 00:07:38,160
make sure your plate isn't exposed to the air for very long. So after each set of streaks,

71
00:07:38,720 --> 00:07:44,400
you should replace your lid very quickly. So once your loop has cooled,

72
00:07:46,080 --> 00:07:48,720
you can touch it to the surface to make sure it's cool enough.

73
00:07:50,080 --> 00:07:55,040
And then turn the plate and intersect your secondary streak lines to create your tertiary

74
00:07:55,040 --> 00:08:03,520
streak lines. Replace the lid and then flame the loop for the final set of streak lines.

75
00:08:14,830 --> 00:08:18,430
So I've turned the plate. I'm going to check that my loop is cool.

76
00:08:19,230 --> 00:08:22,430
And I'm going to intersect the tertiary lines with the quaternary lines.

77
00:08:23,390 --> 00:08:30,190
One, two, three. And now I'm going to flip the loop upside down, drag a vertical line

78
00:08:30,190 --> 00:08:34,510
through those lines and then a zigzag into the middle of the plate so that I get my single

79
00:08:34,510 --> 00:08:41,300
isolated colonies. You need to flame your loop once you've completed the procedure

80
00:08:42,340 --> 00:08:48,020
to make sure you remove any bacteria that might be on there and then place it at the base of

81
00:08:48,020 --> 00:08:54,660
your Bunsen to cool for later on. So now that your plate is complete,

82
00:08:56,180 --> 00:09:02,180
you may be able to see the streak lines when you hold it up to the light. So you should be able

83
00:09:02,180 --> 00:09:07,780
to see your pattern of streak lines. So if you can see streak lines, that means that you have

84
00:09:07,780 --> 00:09:12,420
touched the loop for the surface of the plate and you should get some bacteria growing on there.

85
00:09:14,560 --> 00:09:21,120
For incubation, you need to place your plate upside down. That's to ensure that no condensation

86
00:09:21,120 --> 00:09:26,480
forms on the lid and drops onto the surface of your plate and that can actually contaminate

87
00:09:26,480 --> 00:09:33,200
your sample. So you need to always turn it upside down and put it to one side. If you

88
00:09:33,200 --> 00:09:39,360
are using fungi, there are instances where you don't need to turn your plate upside down but

89
00:09:39,360 --> 00:09:44,720
we'll make you aware of that in class. So that was a demonstration of how to perform

90
00:09:44,720 --> 00:09:50,800
the streak plate technique using a bacteria from a solid surface. Now we're going to do

91
00:09:50,800 --> 00:10:00,530
it with a liquid broth. So here I have some E. coli in a broth. So just give it a quick swirl

92
00:10:00,530 --> 00:10:07,310
to make sure that the cells are suspended throughout the broth. You'll need a new

93
00:10:07,310 --> 00:10:16,140
clean plate and again you'll need to label the plate. So turn your plate upside down and

94
00:10:16,140 --> 00:10:22,380
we're going to write on the circumference of the plate and again this is nutrient agar.

95
00:10:23,180 --> 00:10:29,660
So the abbreviation NA, the initials from your first name, your middle name and your last name

96
00:10:30,460 --> 00:10:38,740
and then the exercise number or the organism. I'm going to write E. coli and even E. coli B.

97
00:10:38,740 --> 00:10:49,020
so I know it's from the broth and also the date. So once again you need to flip your plate

98
00:10:49,660 --> 00:10:56,940
up the correct way. So this is your starting point. You need to re-sterilise your loop

99
00:10:59,620 --> 00:11:06,100
to make sure it's ready to collect some of the broth culture. So because we are

100
00:11:06,900 --> 00:11:12,660
collecting a broth culture you want to make sure that the actual stem is sterile as well

101
00:11:12,660 --> 00:11:17,140
because a large portion of it has to go into the McCartney bottle to collect the culture.

102
00:11:18,900 --> 00:11:25,540
So you need to do this aseptically. So you need to loosen the lid of the McCartney bottle

103
00:11:28,300 --> 00:11:38,340
using the crook of your finger and then flame the neck of the bottle and then insert your sterile

104
00:11:38,340 --> 00:11:44,020
loop. So give your loop a swirl around in the culture to make sure you collect some on your

105
00:11:44,020 --> 00:11:56,080
loop and then you need to re-flame the neck of the bottle and replace the cap. You should be able

106
00:11:56,080 --> 00:12:03,120
to see the broth solution in your loop. It looks like a bubble and you know that you've collected

107
00:12:03,120 --> 00:12:08,720
some of the culture to be transferred. So I'm going to streak this onto

108
00:12:08,800 --> 00:12:17,600
the plate and my starting point is where I've labelled the plate. So this is my primary

109
00:12:19,040 --> 00:12:24,000
street lines. So just drag the loop back and forth off the surface of the plate,

110
00:12:24,800 --> 00:12:32,100
replace the lid and then you need to sterilise your loop. So when performing the street plate

111
00:12:32,100 --> 00:12:38,660
technique, the only time that you'd transfer the inoculum, which is your broth or a colony,

112
00:12:38,660 --> 00:12:44,900
is in the primary, the very first set of streets. You never go back to the bottle or to the colony

113
00:12:44,900 --> 00:12:50,660
and put more onto your loop. Remember the purpose of the street plate technique is to dilute the

114
00:12:50,660 --> 00:12:56,900
number of cells. So you don't want to add more cells to your loop. So now my loop is cool. I'm

115
00:12:56,900 --> 00:13:02,500
going to intercept those primary set of street lines. So I'm going to touch my loop to the

116
00:13:02,500 --> 00:13:07,300
surface to make sure it's cool enough and then intersect the primary street lines,

117
00:13:08,260 --> 00:13:13,780
touching and then lifting off to create four individual street lines on the plate.

118
00:13:15,060 --> 00:13:25,660
I then need to flame the loop again to remove any excess cells and then turn the plate.

119
00:13:28,460 --> 00:13:32,700
So to make sure that my loop is cool, I'm going to touch it to the middle of my plate

120
00:13:34,140 --> 00:13:37,820
and then intersect the secondary lines to create the tertiary lines.

121
00:13:40,820 --> 00:13:47,540
So lifting the loop off after each separate line and then turning the plate ready for the

122
00:13:47,540 --> 00:13:58,190
final set of street lines. So you need to sterilize your loop, allow it to cool

123
00:14:00,110 --> 00:14:07,410
and then perform the final set of street lines. Touch the loop to the surface to make sure it's

124
00:14:07,410 --> 00:14:15,010
cool enough and then perform the lines. For this final step, you can flip your loop upside down

125
00:14:15,010 --> 00:14:22,930
without sterilizing it and create a vertical line and a zigzag to make sure that you get single

126
00:14:22,930 --> 00:14:29,810
colonies coming out into the middle of your plate. You then need to reframe your loop to make sure

127
00:14:29,810 --> 00:14:35,570
that there's no bacteria left on it and place it at the bottom of your Bunsen burner.

128
00:14:37,010 --> 00:14:44,210
Again, this plate is going to be incubated so we compare it with the other plate and again it

129
00:14:44,210 --> 00:14:49,490
must be upside down so that the condensation from the lid doesn't drop onto the surface of the plate

130
00:14:50,210 --> 00:14:54,690
and then you need to rubber band those together with a single rubber band

131
00:14:58,860 --> 00:15:02,780
to make sure that your samples stay together in the incubator.

132
00:15:02,780 --> 00:15:06,300
You then need to place this into the appropriate box for incubation.

133
00:15:08,660 --> 00:15:14,100
When you've completed a technique such as the street plate technique, you must also ensure

134
00:15:14,100 --> 00:15:19,700
that you've cleaned your bench down when you finished the exercise. So that involves turning

135
00:15:19,700 --> 00:15:25,700
off the Bunsen. So make sure you twist it so that the hole is closed on your Bunsen burner and you

136
00:15:25,700 --> 00:15:34,670
see your yellow flame and then it's ready to turn off at the gas. You must also wipe the bench down

137
00:15:34,670 --> 00:15:41,870
just in case any of the culture was spilt or accidentally flipped onto the bench surface when

138
00:15:41,870 --> 00:15:50,340
you were working there and wipe down the disinfectant with a tissue. You can pop that in

139
00:15:50,340 --> 00:15:55,780
the bin. You must also make sure that you wash your hands before you leave the lab.

140
00:15:56,820 --> 00:16:01,780
So now you have the knowledge and have seen how to perform a street plate technique.

141
00:16:01,780 --> 00:16:05,380
So go go forth and street plates.
